href="#sources">Sources</a></nav><a href="/refactor">The Refactor approach</a></aside><article class="refactor-prose"><p class="article-lead">Exchange 1 and 2 among six numbered points. Four points stay put. Now let the same exchange act on six <em>decompositions</em> of the complete graph on those points. All six decompositions move, in three exchanged pairs.</p>
<p>In <a href="/refactors/build-a-commutator">the preceding article</a>, a matching encoded a permutation made of disjoint swaps. Here we use perfect matchings, which pair off <em>all</em> six vertices. A <strong>factorization</strong> is an unordered set of five perfect matchings that uses each edge exactly once. There happen to be six. Call them A through F. A swap of the original labels moves a whole factorization by replacing the labels in each of its five matchings.</p>

      <section class="refactor-matching-experiment" id="try-it" aria-labelledby="experiment-title">
<h2 id="experiment-title">See one swap become three</h2>
<p>Choose two original labels to exchange. Inspect one pair of exchanged factorizations to see what happens to its five matchings. A row such as <span class="cycle">12 | 34 | 56</span> lists three edges, and the shared row stays in place even as the factorizations change places.</p>
